course: dictionary
lesson: 8692
title: Seldom and how often
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Seldom» means not often, or rarely. Read the words around it for clues. «The old man seldom left his house» tells you he did not go out much, so «seldom» must mean rarely.
explain: Here is another clue: «she very seldom followed it», said of Alice's own good advice. She did not often take her own advice, so the meaning fits. Try the swap: "she rarely followed it" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Gorillas show it too: «live mostly on the ground, seldom climbing high». The gorillas do not often climb high. In speech people say «hardly ever» or «not often» instead, but «seldom» is a little more formal, so you will meet it in books and exam passages.
fact: «Seldom» is an adverb, a word that tells you how often something happens.
fact: «Seldom» means not often; rarely.
fact: «Seldom, if ever» means almost never.
fact: In speech people often say «hardly ever» or «not often» instead of «seldom».
fact: In maths and data, something that seldom happens has a low chance of happening.
exercise: «We seldom see snow here.» What does seldom mean? | Very loudly | *Not often | Every day | Always, without fail
exercise: Which phrase means almost never? | *seldom, if ever | at last | quite often | every single day
exercise: «A storm that seldom happens» has what kind of chance of happening? | A high chance | A chance that grows each hour | A certain chance | *A low chance
test: «The old man seldom left his house.» What does this tell you? | He never had a house | He lived in a big castle | He went out every morning | *He rarely went out
test: Which word could replace seldom in «She seldom eats sweets»? | always | *rarely | quickly | loudly
exam: In «Real gorillas ... seldom climbing high», what does seldom tell you? | They climb high only at night | *They rarely climb high | They never touch the ground | They climb high every day
